 many elites within this country  and other countries acting to check and  balance each other in terms of living to  there that's the great challenge  particularly with new technologies what  is happening now and is what people call  the age of super terrorism individuals  just as you as an individual have you  know the power that IBM did not have 40  years ago you know with your PC you have  this incredible power your car has all  of these micro processors in it it's got  more brain power you know then the US  military did in in the Vietnam War you  know in terms of computational ability  but but this can be used as we've seen  for evil as as well as good and up until  now historically the only organizations  that could kill vast numbers of people  were States you know the Roman Empire  you know the Chinese Empire the Ottoman  Empire now you're you have a technology  where a single individual and we're  really focused on these al-qaeda type  terrorists who are dangerous enough but  the real danger or the greater danger  may come from people in the advanced  nations like the United States Europe  and Russia like this anthrax terrorist  who is likely to be somebody actually  from the federal military-industrial  complex who had access to these  high-tech weapons and you know we don't  know what's going to happen there the  Conservatives and the Liberals both have  different answers I think they're  they're both inadequate the conservative  answer is well we'll go out and hunt  them down and smash them you know and  then we'll have peace you know and so we  did this in Afghanistan and it was  necessary and you know that was part of  the campaign but turns out when you  smash them in Afghanistan they start  popping up in Bali Indonesia you know in  Paris France in Rome Italy and you know  here again at some point so it's not a  simple matter of sending out the posse  and finishing them off once and for all  I think the liberal answer in some ways  is a bit more superficial because the  Liberals say well violence and hatred  are caused by poverty so if we we you  know bring the rest of the world up to  American Northern European living  standards at that point people will be  peaceful and free well that just goes  against all of history history shows  that as Aristotle said no tyrant ever  conquered a city because he was cold and  hungry  it's rich people who dream of world  domination I mean if you're a peasant  you know and you're worried that your  cow will die tomorrow you're not  planning to conquer a continent the  struggle in the 20th century was between  the three richest nations of the world  for world domination the British Empire  Germany in the United States it wasn't  you know Honduras you know challenging  Germany and Britain in the u.s. for  world domination so and if you look at  al-qaeda these are not poor people Osama  bin Laden is a prince of the Saudi royal  family  most of these terrorists came from the  very top of the class structure in  countries like Egypt in Saudi Arabia  they were the college educated elite  they were the upper-middle class they  were the professionals these were not  the poor peasants who for obvious  reasons don't organize transnational  business like terrorist networks using  the latest in advanced technology so I  think the Liberals really have to think  twice before they think that merely  giving people more wealth and power it's  not going to release you know the Hitler  is in the osama's who couldn't do  anything when they were poor but now  that they have you know it advanced  economies and advanced weapons can do  something about it well if this  conservative solution of hunting them  down and whacking them doesn't  necessarily work and the liberal  solution of making everybody rich and  powerful doesn't necessarily work well  what does I suspect and I'm in a  pessimistic mind already here that we're  gonna have to change the way we live in  the course of this century I think we're  going to have to accept national  identity cards more intrusive federal  searches I think that you already see  this after September 11th businesses are  going to think twice about putting all  of their employees and their operations  in a single building somewhere I think  we may in much of the industrialized  world return to kind of a quasi medieval  landscape where you have sort of  fortified decentralized structures you  know with electronic and video  surveillance and people shift around  uncomfortably when you say this because  it means you know one of things  President Bush's says these terrorists  will not make us change the way we live  well I think they probably will  I think after after enough terrorism  including terrorism from within you know  from Americans like Timothy McVeigh this  anthrax terrorist if he's an American  I think you know we're going to change  the way we live and accept a degree of  regimentation which those of us who grew  up in in the older world will find you  know quite quite unpleasant but I think  that maybe it's not a solution but it's  a way of ameliorating the danger and one  more question switching parties who do  you think in the democratic party might  be enlightened enough to in the White  House in 2004 well if you had to be  enlightened to win the White House well  as I say I'm not going to handicap the  candidates they're a number of strong  candidates out there on the Democratic  side I will say that governors tend to  do better than senators do you know  governor Bush governor Clinton governor  Carter and there's a reason for this  there's there's some reasons if your  senator particularly if you've been  there for 10 or 20 years you know you've  had to vote on every single  controversial issue that ever existed  snail daughter's you know abortion civil  unions taxes and so on and so you have  all of this thrown at your face when you  run for office if you're a governor you  could have kept diplomatic silence about  a lot of these controversial issues the  second advantage that governors have you  can run against Washington you can say  unlike these senators and congressmen up  there including the ones of my own party  creating this mess you know I ran a  state I balanced the budget or whatever  third governor's have the sort of a  president a governor even of a small  state has the largest coalition of  donors and of party activists who can  man phone lines and get out the vote and  all of that Dwarfs the resources that  all but a few senators can can mobilize  so I think we're now at the stage where  the names being touted the most or US  Senators you you have a couple of  governor's coming in now like Graham  from Florida and Howard Dean from  Vermont write a bill Richardson down the  road but I think what you'll see is as  the election approaches  some of the well-known senators will  fade as they tend to do and some of the  governor's will come from behind and the  fact that you don't know that much about  them is actually an advantage you know I  the reason Abraham Lincoln became  president was it was after all of these  dozens of ballots and they eliminated  every well-known Republican because  precisely because they were well-known  like like uh Seward there were people  who hated them whereas hardly anyone had  ever heard of Lincoln and he hadn't been  in politics except you know in Congress  briefly so nobody hated his guts and he  became a president and a great one well  thank you very much I'll be uh signing  books michael lind is a senior fellow at  the New America Foundation an author of  the next american nation up from  conservatism and vietnam his latest made  in texas is published by basic books on  the web at basic books dot-com Alexander
 

